WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.049
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:09.150
Yes, you see it correctly, this is a paper
on paper.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.280
The paper-paper if you will.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.690
And today, you will witness some amazing works
in the domain of computer graphics and physics

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.690
simulations.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.300
There is so much progress in this area.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:30.430
For instance, we can simulate honey coiling,
baking and melting, bouncy jelly and many

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.320
related phenomena.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.739
And none of these techniques use any machine
learning, these are all good old-fashined

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:39.969
handcrafted algorithms.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:44.989
And using these, we can even simulate stretching
and compression, to the point that muscle

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:47.699
movement simulations are possible.

00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:53.840
When attaching the muscles to bones, as we
move the character, the muscles move and contract

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:54.840
accurately.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.979
What’s more, this work can even perform
muscle growth simulations.

00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:02.229
So, are we done here?

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:07.930
Did these ingenious computer graphics researchers
max out physics simulations, where there is

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.470
nothing else to do?

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.920
Oh no, of course not!

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:18.180
Look, this footage is from an earlier graphics
paper that simulates viscosity and melting

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:24.330
fluids, and what I would like you to look
at here is not what it does, but what it doesn’t

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.330
do.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:31.500
It starts melting these Armadillos beautifully,
however, what there is something that it doesn’t

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.930
do, which is, mixing.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:38.130
The materials start separate, and remain separate.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.910
Can we improve upon that somehow?

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:47.140
Well, this new paper promises that and so
much more that it truly makes my head spin.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:53.550
For instance, it can simulate hyperelastic,
elastoplastic, viscous, fracturing and multiphase

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:59.820
coupling behaviors, and most importantly,
all of these can be simulated within the same

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:00.820
framework.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:06.400
Not one paper for each behavior, one paper
that can do all of these.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.520
That is absolutely insane.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:10.759
What does all that mean?

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:17.349
Well, I say, let’s see them all right now
through 5 super fun experiments.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:18.849
Experiment number one.

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:20.159
Wet papers.

00:02:20.159 --> 00:02:23.379
As you see, this technique handles the ball
of water.

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:26.260
Okay, we’ve seen that before.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.650
And what else?

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:34.120
Well, it handles the paper too, okay, that’s
getting better, but, hold on to your papers,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.069
and look, it also handles the water’s interaction
with the paper.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:41.099
Now we’re talking!

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:46.319
And careful with holding on to that paper,
because if you do it correctly, this might

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:47.319
happen.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:52.459
As you see, the arguments contained within
this paper really hold water.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:55.139
Experiment number two, fracturing.

00:02:55.139 --> 00:03:00.290
As you know, most computer graphics papers
on physics simulation contain creative solutions

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.840
to destroying Armadillos in the most spectacular
fashion.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.689
This work, is, of course, no different.

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:09.739
Yum.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:11.099
Experiment number three.

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:12.099
Dissolution.

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:19.819
Here, we take a glass of water, add some starch
powder, it starts piling up, and then, slowly

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:21.730
starts to dissolve.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:27.620
And note that the water itself also becomes
stickier during the process.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.779
Number four.

00:03:28.779 --> 00:03:29.779
Dipping.

00:03:29.779 --> 00:03:33.939
We first take a piece of biscuit, and dip
it into the water.

00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:39.400
Note that the coupling works correctly here,
in other words, the water now moves, but what

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.829
is even better is that the biscuit started
absorbing some of that water.

00:03:44.829 --> 00:03:51.099
And now, when we rip it apart, oh yes.

00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:52.209
Excellent!

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:56.669
And as a light transport researcher by trade,
I love watching the shape of the biscuits

00:03:56.669 --> 00:04:00.480
distorted here due to the refraction of the
water.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.900
This is a beautiful demonstration of that
phenomenon.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.780
And, number five.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.239
The dog!

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:08.999
What kind of dog you ask?

00:04:08.999 --> 00:04:15.620
Well, this virtual dog gets a big splash of
water, starts shaking it off, and manages

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.530
to get rid of most of it.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:20.000
But only most of it.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.810
And it can do all of these, using one algorithm.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:31.280
Not one per each of these beautiful phenomena,
one technique can perform all of these.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.970
That is absolutely amazing.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:38.949
But it does not stop there, it can also simulate
snow, and it not only does it well, but it

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:41.050
does that swiftly.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.520
How swiftly?

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.770
It simulated this a bit faster than one frame
per second.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.200
The starch powder experiment was about one
minute per frame, and the slowest example

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.669
was the dog shaking off the ball of water.

00:04:54.669 --> 00:05:00.000
The main reason for this is that it required
near a quarter million particles of water

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.650
and for hair, and when the algorithm computes
these interactions between them, it can only

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.050
advance the time in very small increments.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:14.430
It has to do this a hundred thousand times
for each second of footage that you see here.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.310
Based on how much computation there is to
do, that is really, really fast.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:24.070
And, don’t forget that the First Law Of
Papers says that research is a process.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:29.259
Do not look at where we are, look at where
we will be two more papers down the line.

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:34.270
And even now, the generality of this system
is truly something to behold.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:37.169
Congratulations to the authors on this amazing
paper.

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:39.580
What a time to be alive!

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:45.280
So, if you wish to read a beautifully written
paper today that does not dissolve in your

00:05:45.280 --> 00:06:00.830
hands, I highly 

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:38.450
recommend this one.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:42.400
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

